+        </div>
+
+        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
+
+        <div class="faq">
+            <details>
+                <summary>How long does the Amazon Best Seller badge last?</summary>
+                <p>There is no fixed duration. The badge is shown while your book is ranked #1 in a category, and Amazon recalculates sales rank roughly every hour. In books we monitor, a badge has stayed up for days but has also disappeared within a single hour. One business book lost and regained its badge six times in under three days.</p>
+            </details>
+            <details>
+                <summary>Why did my Amazon bestseller badge disappear?</summary>
+                <p>Another book outsold yours in that category during the last ranking window, so it took the #1 spot and the badge moved with it. Sales rank is relative, so a quiet hour for you or a strong hour for a competitor is enough. Amazon does not notify you when this happens.</p>
+            </details>
+            <details>
+                <summary>How long does the #1 New Release badge last?</summary>
+                <p>The #1 New Release badge is only available while your book counts as a new release, roughly the first month after publication, and within that window it follows the same hour-by-hour ranking as the Best Seller badge. Once the new-release window closes the badge is gone for good, regardless of sales.</p>
+            </details>
+            <details>
+                <summary>Can I get the bestseller badge back after it disappears?</summary>
+                <p>Yes, if your book returns to #1 in the category. The badge is not a one-time award; it reflects the current ranking. Books we track regularly regain a badge hours or days after losing it, which is also why it is worth capturing every time it appears.</p>
+            </details>
+            <details>
+                <summary>How do I prove my book was an Amazon bestseller?</summary>
+                <p>A timestamped screenshot of the product page showing the badge and the category is the accepted proof. Amazon keeps no public history of who held a badge and when, so if you did not capture it while it was visible there is no way to retrieve it later.</p>
+            </details>
+        </div>
